From 46dddaef8e8bac164a3d1a29d1164c53b8b30daa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: FiyeroT <me@fiyero.xyz>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 17:43:29 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 101/118] decode-link: build the outbound to validate it,
 refuse by a table of reasons

The command now builds the outbound it is about to print the way "sing-box check"
builds a configuration, as the only outbound of one. What is printed is therefore an
outbound this very sing-box takes: with the protocols it was built with, the options
its line has and the checks of the outbound itself. The decoder's own tables of what
the outbound would refuse are gone.

The decoder writes the options by name into an ordered tree, so that its text does not
depend on the option structures of the sing-box it is built into.

A link is refused only when the server cannot be reached without what it asks for;
every such refusal has an ID and a row in a table with what it rests on (the interop
stand against Xray-core, or the code). What the client alone decides is a warning:

- gRPC mode multi: gun is used, the server answers both; a custom path maps only as
  /name/Tun, the one shape sing-box can say;
- the HTTP header of the TCP transport without TLS is the http transport; with TLS it
  is refused;
- a fingerprint only Xray knows gives the one of the same browser; one nobody knows,
  no imitation, as in Xray (REALITY refuses both);
- a value of encryption that is not VLESS Encryption reads as none;
- a 32-digit UUID without dashes is read as Xray reads it;
- finalmask: fragment and noise are the client's own, any other mask is refused;
- ech is mapped (a configuration list, or "ask DNS"); a pinned certificate gets a
  warning that says what follows;
- ws without host takes the server name of TLS, as Xray does; httpupgrade drops the
  early data of its path;
- a flow over ws, grpc, http and httpupgrade is refused, as over xhttp, and so is one
  without TLS;
- tor, ssh, shadowtls and snell are named as having no link form.

Error messages no longer repeat a range value they could not read, nor name a type of
the program.
